From c29da0700169335a072932300ad082a2305220a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aquarat <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:23:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: avd: allow cacheable (non-coherent) MMAP
 buffers

The capture buffers are allocated with vb2_dma_contig_memops on a device
that is not marked dma-coherent, so both the kernel and the user space
mapping are Normal-NC (pgprot_dmacoherent()). Reading decoded frames back
through such a mapping is extremely slow on Apple silicon: every load is
a serialised uncached DRAM transaction, and neither wider loads nor
non-temporal loads help.

Measured on an M1 Mac mini (T8103) with one 2304x1296 NV12 capture
buffer (13.2 MB plane, 4.5 MB of it image data) mapped by user space:

  mapping                    memcpy   NEON ld1x4  ldnp/stnp  read-only u64
  coherent (Normal-NC)      240 MB/s   250 MB/s   246 MB/s    124 MB/s
  non-coherent (cacheable) 17.9 GB/s  23.8 GB/s  23.9 GB/s   18.8 GB/s

For a client that copies the frames to system memory (ffmpeg's
v4l2request hwaccel without a DRM-PRIME consumer, as used by Frigate)
that is ~18 ms of CPU time per 2304x1296 frame just for the copy, which
made hardware decode cost 4-5x more CPU than software decode.

Let user space opt into cacheable buffers with the standard videobuf2
mechanism: set allow_cache_hints on both queues so that
V4L2_MEMORY_FLAG_NON_COHERENT (REQBUFS/CREATE_BUFS) and
V4L2_BUF_FLAG_NO_CACHE_{INVALIDATE,CLEAN} (QBUF) are honoured, and
advertise V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_MMAP_CACHE_HINTS. videobuf2 then
allocates with dma_alloc_noncontiguous(), maps the buffers cacheable and
does the cache maintenance in prepare/finish (clean before the hardware
runs, invalidate at DQBUF), so the data is still correct without any
assumption about the fabric's coherency.

DMA_ATTR_NO_KERNEL_MAPPING has to go from the capture queue:
dma_alloc_noncontiguous() rejects every attribute other than
DMA_ATTR_ALLOC_SINGLE_PAGES (WARN_ON_ONCE in kernel/dma/mapping.c) and
the allocation fails with -ENOMEM. The attribute was ineffective anyway:
with an IOMMU and a blocking allocation iommu_dma_alloc() always goes
through iommu_dma_alloc_remap(), which creates the vmap mapping
regardless of the attribute.

Clients that do not pass the flag are unaffected (same coherent
allocation and mapping as before). With ffmpeg requesting
V4L2_MEMORY_FLAG_NON_COHERENT for its capture buffers, the
Frigate-like chain "-hwaccel v4l2request -i 2304x1296.h264 -vf
fps=5,scale=640:360 -f rawvideo -pix_fmt yuv420p" for 150 frames goes
from 2.97 s user + 0.09 s sys to 0.36-0.40 s user + 0.11-0.14 s sys
(software decode: 0.53 s user single-threaded, 0.76 s with the default
thread count), output bit-identical. Decode + read-back alone
("-f null -") goes from 2.75 s to 0.08 s user + 0.12 s sys, the
remaining sys time being the cache maintenance over the 13.2 MB planes.
Verified with the full clip matrix (gst v4l2slh264dec, coherent buffers,
unchanged) and repeated per-frame md5 comparison against software decode
with the non-coherent buffers.

Signed-off-by: <fill in>
---
 avd-drv.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-drv.c
index abbe6d4..47e8ef7 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/avd/avd-drv.c
@@ -263,6 +263,7 @@ static int avd_queue_init(void *priv, struct vb2_queue *src_vq,
 	src_vq->lock = &ctx->dev->vdev_lock;
 	src_vq->dev = ctx->dev->v4l2_dev.dev;
 	src_vq->supports_requests = true;
+	src_vq->allow_cache_hints = true;
 
 	ret = vb2_queue_init(src_vq);
 	if (ret)
@@ -270,7 +271,7 @@ static int avd_queue_init(void *priv, struct vb2_queue *src_vq,
 
 	dst_vq->bidirectional = true;
 	dst_vq->mem_ops = &vb2_dma_contig_memops;
-	dst_vq->dma_attrs = DMA_ATTR_NO_KERNEL_MAPPING;
+	dst_vq->dma_attrs = 0;
 	dst_vq->type = V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE_MPLANE;
 	dst_vq->io_modes = VB2_MMAP | VB2_DMABUF;
 	dst_vq->drv_priv = ctx;
@@ -279,6 +280,7 @@ static int avd_queue_init(void *priv, struct vb2_queue *src_vq,
 	dst_vq->timestamp_flags = V4L2_BUF_FLAG_TIMESTAMP_COPY;
 	dst_vq->lock = &ctx->dev->vdev_lock;
 	dst_vq->dev = ctx->dev->v4l2_dev.dev;
+	dst_vq->allow_cache_hints = true;
 
 	return vb2_queue_init(dst_vq);
 }
